@*
    The payment simulator's control panel, on the checkout page where a reviewer will look for
    it.

    THIS IS THE CONTROL THAT MAKES THE DEMO A DEMO. Anyone can build a checkout that works. The
    interesting paths are a refused card, a settlement that arrives seconds later, a webhook
    delivered twice, a webhook delivered in the wrong order — and none of them can be shown to a
    reviewer who has no way to ask for them. Hiding this behind a magic basket total (which the
    simulator also supports, and which is documented in the disclosure below) would mean the
    demo's best material only reaches people who read the source first.

    It is a plain radio group rather than a select, because the point is that the six paths are
    visible at once with a sentence each. The summaries are written for a shopper: what happens
    to the money, what happens to the cart, what happens to the reserved stock.

    Nothing here is a secret from the server: the value is sent as `paymentScenario` and the API
    passes it to the gateway port as an opaque hint, which a real gateway adapter would ignore.
*@
@using VelaCommerce.Storefront.Checkout

<fieldset class="vc-scenarios">
    <legend class="vc-scenarios__legend">Payment simulator</legend>

    <p class="vc-scenarios__intro">
        This shop runs its own payment gateway, so there is no card to enter and
        <strong>nothing is ever charged</strong>. Choose what you would like the gateway to do
        and the rest of the order behaves exactly as it would in production &mdash; the same
        signed settlement notifications, the same state machine, the same background workers.
    </p>

    <div class="vc-scenarios__list">
        @foreach (var option in PaymentScenarios.All)
        {
            var id = $"vc-scenario-{option.Hint}";

            <div class="vc-scenario @(IsSelected(option) ? "vc-scenario--selected" : null)">
                <input class="vc-scenario__input"
                       type="radio"
                       id="@id"
                       name="vc-payment-scenario"
                       value="@option.Hint"
                       checked="@IsSelected(option)"
                       disabled="@Disabled"
                       aria-describedby="@($"{id}-summary")"
                       @onchange="() => SelectAsync(option)" />
                <label class="vc-scenario__label" for="@id">@option.Label</label>
                <p class="vc-scenario__summary" id="@($"{id}-summary")">@option.Summary</p>
            </div>
        }
    </div>

    <details class="vc-scenarios__detail">
        <summary>Triggering these without this page</summary>
        <p class="vc-scenarios__detail-body">
            The simulator checks the <code class="vc-code">paymentScenario</code> hint first and
            the order total second, so every path is reachable from a plain HTTP client with no
            extra field to discover. Only the last two minor units of the total are read, which
            is why $1.01, $47.01 and $1,203.01 all decline.
        </p>
        <dl class="vc-scenarios__triggers">
            @foreach (var option in PaymentScenarios.All)
            {
                <div class="vc-scenarios__trigger">
                    <dt><code class="vc-code">@option.Hint</code></dt>
                    <dd class="vc-tabular">@option.AmountTrigger</dd>
                </div>
            }
        </dl>
    </details>
</fieldset>

@code {
    /// <summary>The selected scenario's hint. Matched case-insensitively, so a value from configuration still selects a radio.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public string Value { get; set; } = PaymentScenarios.DefaultHint;

    /// <summary>Raised with the newly chosen hint.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<string> ValueChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Locks the group while a checkout is in flight, so the scenario cannot change under an attempt that has already been sent.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Disabled { get; set; }

    private bool IsSelected(PaymentScenarioOption option) =>
        string.Equals(option.Hint, Value, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase);

    private Task SelectAsync(PaymentScenarioOption option) => ValueChanged.InvokeAsync(option.Hint);
}
